{{/*
doc_comment renders the prose half of a declaration's doc comment: "title" and
"description", in the two shapes Go documentation has.

Go's convention is a comment that opens with the identifier being declared and
whose first paragraph summarises it, with the detail below. JSON Schema writes
those two things as two keywords -- "title" is a short label and "description"
the explanation -- so where a schema states both they map onto the convention
exactly: the title becomes the summary line and the description the paragraph
under it, separated by the blank comment line that makes them two paragraphs
rather than one run-on sentence.

Where a schema states one of them, that one is the whole comment and takes the
opening line, so a type documented only by its description keeps the comment it
has always had, byte for byte. Before issue #188 a title reached neither shape at
any position but the root's name, and a schema whose only prose was a title
declared a type with no comment at all.

The two definitions differ only in whether the identifier is named. A type's
comment opens with its name because `go doc` and every tool built on it read the
comment as a sentence about that name; a struct member's does not, because the
member is already spelled on the line below and this generator has never put it
there. The indent is the member's, and it is on the continuation lines too: a
description holding a newline becomes several comment lines, and one of them
starting at column zero inside a struct body is not what gofmt would have
written.
*/}}
{{define "type_doc"}}
{{- if .Title}}
// {{.Name}} - {{comment "" .Title}}
{{- if .Description}}
//
// {{comment "" .Description}}
{{- end}}
{{- else if .Description}}
// {{.Name}} - {{comment "" .Description}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{define "member_doc"}}
{{- if .Title}}
	// {{comment "\t" .Title}}
{{- if .Description}}
	//
	// {{comment "\t" .Description}}
{{- end}}
{{- else if .Description}}
	// {{comment "\t" .Description}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
